In a horizontal-tb column flex container, Ladybird now measures
baselines across the horizontal axis: an item in a vertical writing mode
by its own baselines, any other item by one synthesized from its left
border edge; and vertical-rl items share a group at the right edge.
That’s what Blink does: DetermineBaselineWritingMode() in
baseline_utils.h gives a horizontal-tb item vertical-lr in an ltr
container, LogicalBoxFragment::SynthesizedBaseline() puts its alphabetic
baseline on the line-under (left) edge, and DetermineBaselineGroup()
puts vertical-rl items in the minor group, which
FlexLayoutAlgorithm::BaselineAscent() measures from each item’s
cross-end margin edge.

Under rtl and under wrap-reverse, all three engines align a
horizontal-tb item at the cross-start edge, as Ladybird now does, and
none lets an item with an auto cross-axis margin take part in baseline
alignment, as the flexbox algorithm says.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5
(Playwright’s build), all three agree with Ladybird now on the test’s
rows with real or synthesized horizontal-tb baselines, an auto margin,
rtl, and wrap-reverse. On the rows of empty vertical-lr and vertical-rl
boxes, Chrome and WebKit agree with each other and with Ladybird, and
Firefox synthesizes differently; on a vertical-rl item under rtl, Chrome
and Firefox agree with Ladybird, and WebKit differs. Ladybird still
differs from Chrome and WebKit for vertical text, which they align by
its central baseline, and Ladybird by its alphabetic one.
